Step 1: Image Collection
Tank and artillery images were collected from publicly available online sources.
Images were selected with different angles, lighting conditions, and backgrounds to improve dataset diversity.

Step 2: Image Annotation
All collected images were manually annotated using labeImg.
Bounding boxes were drawn around tanks and artillery objects.
Annotations were exported in YOLO format (.txt files).

Step 3: Dataset Organization
The annotated dataset was organized into training and validation folders.

dataset/
   train/
      images/
      labels/
   val/
      images/
      labels/

A dataset configuration file (data.yaml) was created to define class names and dataset paths.

Step 4: Model Selection
YOLOv8 pretrained model was selected as the base model.

Step 5: Model Training
The model was trained using the custom dataset.
Training parameters such as epochs, batch size, and image size were configured.
GPU acceleration was enabled for faster training.

Step 6: Model Evaluation
The trained model was validated on unseen images.

Step 7: Integration of Person Detection
A pretrained YOLO model was used for detecting persons.
Outputs from the custom tank-artillery model and pretrained person model were combined during inference.

Step 8: Object Tracking
A tracking algorithm (byte track) was integrated to assign unique IDs to detected objects across frames.

Step 9: Video Inference
The combined detection and tracking pipeline was applied to video input.
The system detected tanks, artillery, and persons simultaneously in real time.

Step 10: Visualization
Bounding boxes, class labels, confidence scores, and tracking IDs were drawn on video frames.

